catalog.contentShape:
The game consumes a DECK: gridSize/2 pair-units (or gridSize/3 triple-units) built by code from pack items.

Each card: {no (1-30 or 'A1'-'E6'), unitId, side 'a'|'b'|'c', text?, imageKey?, tts (the text to speak), itemRef}. A unit is {unitId, rel, cards[2|3], model (example sentence), whyTr?, defTr?, ipa?, lockCheck ('say'|'frame'|'otherForm'|'spell'|'opposite'|'sentence'|'context'), frame? (a sentence starter for Say it / Lock it), actable?}.

Sources:
- pair items are used directly: a, b, rel (tr|emoji|def|syn|ant|colloc|qa|half|past|idiom-meaning|sound), aMedia/bMedia, and set (triples = pairs sharing a set, e.g. go-went + went-gone become go / went / gone).
- vocab items are converted: term ↔ media.emoji/img (picture-word), term ↔ defEasy (A) or def (B, 18 words or fewer, only if it fits the card cap), term ↔ defTr[0] (EN-TR), gap.text ↔ term ('complete me' card), and family[] → triples. example becomes the model sentence, ipa goes to TTS, and actable sets the act-it moment.
- sound items give rhyme and homophone pairs (sea / see), and sound cards (tts only, with a speaker icon) paired with pictures.
- odd items feed the 'What's missing?' / 'Double Vision' interlude.
- chance-card items (kind reward/bonus/task, task act|spell|sing|speak) supply the text for the power pairs; their effects are clamped by the engine and filtered by harshness.

LLM path: the teacher's free prompt (Turkish is fine) goes to the generator with the merged AudienceProfile (level caps, word band, teenSafe, picture requirement), which returns pair items with rel and model sentences. Code builds the deck.

VALIDATOR (uniqueness first; every card must have exactly one partner in the grid):
- no duplicate faces;
- no shared TR gloss;
- each collocation head and each phrasal particle-verb appears once per grid, and every tail is checked against the other heads (an LLM second pass asks 'which other cards in this grid could this card match?' and drops the conflicts);
- synonym and antonym cross-checks against words.js families and definitions;
- homophone collisions;
- distinct pictures (no cat plus kitten, no two red objects in a colours grid);
- card-face word caps per level and audience;
- the teenSafe filter for Genç;
- an imageKey is required on at least one side for A1/A2 Genç, and unpicturable items are dropped.

If fewer valid units than needed remain, the deck is topped up from the nearest built-in category, or the grid shrinks with a notice ('16 yerine 14 kart'). A failed LLM call falls back to the built-in bank.

Built-in categories:
- Kids: YLE / MEB topics with pictures: animals, food, colours, body, clothes, school things, toys, rooms, weather, hobbies, transport, family, actions, numbers, opposites, rhymes, plus MEB grade 2-8 units.
- Adults: travel, hotel, restaurant, work, make / do / take / have, phrasal verbs, irregular verbs, idioms, false friends, word families, small talk Q-A, email phrases.

The game consumes one content pack (built-in or LLM-generated) in two layers.

LAYER 1: STORY FRAME
pack.frames.story is made of 'story-node' items: {title, titleTr, skin ('fantasy'|'space'|'jungle'|'pirates'|'city'|'office'|'travel'|'mystery'), nodes[], endings {win, lose, funny}}.

Each node has these fields:
- id
- act (1-3)
- kind ('intro'|'gate'|'npc'|'riddle'|'trap'|'chest'|'fork'|'shop'|'camp'|'boss'|'ending')
- narration (1-3 panels, each within the group's word cap)
- npcName?
- npcLine? (for the boss, one taunt per phase)
- choices[{label (one sentence, within the cap), to, effects?{hp, gold, item}}]
- slot? {types[], skill ('words'|'grammar'|'talk'|'listen'|'read'), count, diff 1-3}
- outcomes? {success, fail} (fail is the funny complication line; the repair line is the slotted item's own right answer)
- scene (a background or sticker key)

Boss phases are 2-3 chained 'boss' nodes. Event cards are 'chance-card' items. Mystery skins add pack.frames.case: 4-6 suspects with attrs, clues by round (revealed as Chest loot), culpritId, confession, and mature=false for Genç.

CODE OWNS THE TOPOLOGY. The code decides:
- ids, edges, kinds, acts, and the difficulty per path;
- the node count (from the lesson minutes);
- the hero cards per skin and audience, with magic sentences graded by level;
- the shop list and prices, and the backpack items;
- the event deck, filtered by the harshness level;
- the key-word list (6-8 vocab items);
- the 'Previously on...' recap and the Adventure Log, both templated from the run log at level.
The LLM writes ONLY the narration, npcLine, choice labels, outcome lines, boss taunts and endings for the node list it is given, with the whole outline in context.

LAYER 2: ANSWERABLE ITEMS THAT FILL THE SLOTS
The code assigns items by type and diff, so a dropped item never breaks the story.
- gate (Challenge / creature):
  - mcq (fmt picture | meaning | gap | context | dialogue);
  - vocab (a picture MCQ built from media + distractors; defEasy to word; TR to EN at A levels; the example gap);
  - gap (with a bank for Genç and A groups);
  - qa (typed answers at B levels);
  - sound (a listening gate: a minimal pair, 'Which word do you hear?', tts).
- riddle (Puzzle Door):
  - riddle (clues[3], hard to easy);
  - vocab spell (hangman-eligible terms; letter tiles for Genç);
  - lock (derive: firstLetters of the last answered items; hints[3]; the code is computed, never written by the LLM).
- trap:
  - error (wrong / right / span / whyTr);
  - tf (about 'sentence-correct', with a fix).
- chest:
  - pair (a 4-card match; rel tr / emoji / def / syn / past / idiom-meaning);
  - order (a sentence or events; chunked tokens for Genç);
  - odd;
  - category-list (sort 6 members into 2 labels).
- npc (Talk):
  - roleplay (roleA = the spotlight team's goals and useful[]; roleB = the teacher's GM script; the checklist drives Great / Good / Weak);
  - speaking-prompt (mode ask | describe | opinion | debate; starters are shown as frames; secs sets the planning and talk time);
  - taboo-card (mode describe or act, for the Bard's 'describe it without the word' and for Oyun Parkı TPR nodes).
- fork, and every Yol Ayrımı scene: a comprehension gate on the scene, either tf (about 'text') or mcq (fmt 'reading') with textRef. B levels may add a text item as a clue page (glossary of at most 5 words, evidence map).
- camp: re-serves the items the class missed ('Kaçırılanlar'), of any type.
- boss:
  - phase 1: mcq or vocab;
  - phase 2: gap, error or order;
  - phase 3: speaking-prompt (a debate at B2).
  In Oyun Parkı: vocab with actable=true for the TPR finishing move.
- event: chance-card (kind reward | penalty | task | swap | bonus; task speak | act | spell | sing; amount clamped by the engine; textTr for A1 Genç).

AMOUNTS
- Hızlı Macera: at least 16 answerable items. Bölüm: at least 24. Büyük Bölüm: at least 36.
- The diff mix is about 40 / 40 / 20 across 1 / 2 / 3. The Kolay / Orta / Zor dial shifts it, and the hard path pulls diff 3.
- Oyun Parkı: every item needs media.emoji or an imageKey. Items that cannot be pictured are dropped from picture gates.

BUILT-IN SOURCES
- grammar_mcq_a1..c1 (mcq; whyOpt feeds whyTr);
- words.js (vocab: defs, TR glosses and examples for gaps; IPA for sound items);
- readings_* (text plus tf/mcq);
- idioms.js (B2 idiom-meaning pairs).

PROMPT MODES
(1) 'Sadece sorular' (the default; cheap and safe): the LLM generates items for the teacher's topic or Turkish prompt, fitted to a built-in skeleton.
(2) 'Bu paketten hikâye': the LLM also writes the narration layer for a code-built outline.

VALIDATOR
- Word caps per panel.
- The lexical band, checked against words.js.
- The teenSafe blocklist: never kill, blood, die, weapon, alcohol, dating, gambling or horror; the story verbs are befriend, calm, freeze, shrink, make laugh and reboot.
- No brands.
- Every choice.to exists, every node is reachable, there is at least one win ending, and a depth cap holds.
A failing node falls back to the skeleton's template line. The teacher previews and edits every node before play.

Code builds a board POOL from vocab items grouped into clusters. The pool is 1.4x the board size (16 → 23, 20 → 28, 25 → 35), so any card can be swapped.

CARD = vocab item:
- term: 1 word, at most 12 characters for projector legibility; 2 words only on B2 idiom boards;
- alt[] and family[], which feed the clue validator;
- pos;
- media.imageKey or emoji, required for A1/A2 Genç (unpicturable items are dropped);
- defEasy, def (B groups), defTr, example and concrete, which feed the card-help ladder and the debrief.

CLUSTERS = category-list items {label, labelTr, members}. Each board draws 3-5 clusters of 4-6 members, so multi-card clues always exist. At B1+ it adds 3-6 BRIDGE words that sit in two clusters (bank: money/river; light: lamp/weight; match: fire/football; spring: season/water). These give the real Codenames 'aha'.

RIDDLE items {answer, clues[3] from hard to easy, each within the group's word cap} power the HQ/Gogo description clues, the kids' second-try clue and the Arena Backup hint.

PAIR items with rel 'colloc' or 'syn' (heavy–rain, heavy–traffic) power HQ one-word clues at B1+ and check the B2 constraint rounds.

LLM (free prompt) returns only raw material:
- clusters[{label, labelTr, members[{term, emoji, defEasy, defTr}]}]
- bridges[{term, clusters[2]}]
- riddles[{answer, clues[3]}]
- collocations[{a, b}]
The LLM never places words on the board or builds the key.

CODE then:
- checks each term against the words.js band (at or below level+1; unknown words are flagged for the teacher);
- dedupes by lemma, family[], alt[], plural, identical Turkish gloss, identical emoji, and substring compounds (sun/sunflower, fire/fireman);
- runs teenSafe for every Genç group;
- enforces the text caps on riddle clues;
- picks the board.

KEY: seeded from the 5-character key code. A balancer rerolls it (max 50 tries) until:
- each team has 2 or more clusters that each hold 2 or more of its agents;
- the two teams' linkability differs by 1 or less;
- the trap sits in a cluster with agents at B1+ (tempting, so it matters), but next to 1 or fewer of the active team's agents in Oyun Parkı (dragon kindness).

The class profile stores the board seed, the key code and the clue log for the debrief.

catalog.adultVersion:
STÜDYO: 'SORT DESK'

LOOK. An editorial quiz-night look, in cream or the dark 'Karartılmış sınıf' theme, set in system sans with no mascot and no voice lines. The caption reads neutrally ('Leg 2 · Linkers'). Team names come from cities, coffee drinks or grammar puns ('The Irregular Verbs').
- SORT bins are manila folders with coloured tabs.
- BUILD is a composing stick: tiles like metal type slide in with a woodblock 'clack', and a correct sentence 'prints' onto a card with a letterpress impression.
- RANK is a vertical list beside a gradient scale bar.

RULES
- The default is Check at the end. Each runner makes one move or one fix.
- 'Kontrol' shows only the count of correct cards. Each runner then gets one fix, and the reveal gives a one-line why for every wrong card.
- Optional 15 s cap per move, or a heat timer of 90-120 s.
- Points: 2 for first try, 1 after a fix. Optional -1 for a card still wrong. Optional 'Clean call' wager (+5/-3).
- 'Objection!' (once per heat): a waiting team claims that a placed card is wrong. If they are right, they get +2 and the card rebounds to them.
- Hint ladder: the rule (e.g. 'Adverbs of frequency go before the main verb') costs 1, a model example costs 1, a Turkish gloss costs 2. The TR gloss can be switched off for immersion classes.
- Pair consultation and a rotating spokesperson are the defaults. Classes that prefer not to walk play Caller mode from their seats ('Desk Relay').

CONTENT
- SORT:
  - register: formal / neutral / informal ('I would be grateful if you could...' / 'Could you...?' / 'Can you...?');
  - linkers by function: contrast / addition / result / example;
  - make / do / take / have collocations;
  - -ed endings /t/ /d/ /ɪd/;
  - word-family suffixes;
  - gerund vs infinitive at B2, with a 'Both, but the meaning changes' Venn bin (stop, remember, try).
- BUILD:
  - adverb placement ('She has hardly ever been late');
  - indirect questions ('Could you tell me where the station is?');
  - inversion at B2;
  - dialogue order for a complaint phone call;
  - paragraph order for a cover letter.
- RANK:
  - certainty: definitely > probably > might > probably not > definitely not;
  - politeness of requests;
  - adjective strength: good < great < amazing;
  - steps in a visa or job application.
- OPEN RANK: 'What matters most in a job offer?' (salary, commute, flexibility, team, training), scored with the reason rubric.

ENDING. Full scores, then the 'Kaçırılanlar' list: every bounced card and every wrong order, with the right answer and why. The list can be exported to the Material Maker as a homework worksheet.

TIMING. 20-30 min as a full 3-leg match, or 5-8 min as a single-leg warm-up.

catalog.contentShape:
pack.frames.survival is a story envelope extended with meters:
- title, titleTr
- skin: 'jungle' | 'space' | 'city' | 'office' | 'pirates' | 'travel'
- unit: 'day' | 'block' | 'month'
- days: 3-7, set by lesson-fit
- meters[4]: {id, label, labelTr, icon, start}
- signal: {label, icon}
- roles[4-6]: {id, label, meter, power?}
- salvage[8-12]: {ref (a vocab item id with media), expertRank (must be a permutation; validated), why (EN, within the level cap), whyTr, unlocks[] (option ids)}
- nodes[]: story-node items of these kinds:
  - 'intro'
  - 'fork' (a day event): narration, scene, roles[] (the crews it touches), and choices[]. Each choice has: id, label, labelTr?, effects {food, water, health, morale, signal}, hidden?, odds? (0.3-0.7, B1+ only), requires? {item | flag}, sets? (flag), and diary (a past-simple line, 'We ...'). The LLM writes effects as tokens ('--', '-', '0', '+', '++'); code turns them into numbers for the difficulty and clamps them to ±20 per meter (±1 notch in Oyun Parkı).
  - 'camp' (night)
  - 'npc' (a visitor with a trade)
  - 'boss' (the finale: location clues for the radio call)
  - 'ending'
- endings: {win, lose, funny}

Other item types the game uses:
- chance-card: night cards and crisis cards. Amounts are clamped by the engine.
- speaking-prompt: mode 'debate', 'hypothetical' or 'opinion'. Its starters form the council phrase strip for the level.
- The challenge pool: any answerable pack items (vocab, mcq, gap, qa, tf, order, odd, riddle, error, pair), plus text with questionIds for the bottle message and taboo-card for Explorer reports.
- roleplay (optional): the B1+ radio-call finale.

Built-in content: 4 kids, 4 Arena and 5 adult scenarios, with 14-18 events each.

LLM generation: the teacher's Turkish prompt produces the whole envelope. The validator checks:
- the word caps and the teenSafe list;
- that every option has a diary line;
- that expertRank is a permutation;
- that no option is strictly best on every meter;
- that no event depends on an unpicturable item for A1/A2 Genç (such events are dropped).
If a check fails, the game falls back to built-in events.
